            "heading": "How to enjoy it",
            "icon": "none",
            "content": "<p><strong>Neat, first.</strong> Pour a measure and give it a minute — a good Scotch says most of what it has to say before you add anything.</p><p><strong>Then a drop of water.</strong> Still, room-temperature water opens up the nose and softens the spirit. Add it slowly; you can't take it back out.</p><p><strong>A good glass helps.</strong> Something tulip-shaped draws the aromas towards you — a tumbler looks the part but lets them drift off.</p><p><strong>Storing it.</strong> Upright, out of direct sun, cap tight. It won't age in the bottle the way wine does, but it's not frozen in time either — a fresh blend can take a little while to marry, and a half-finished bottle will drift a touch over the months. Nothing to fuss over.</p>",
